    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1044">

                <pb facs="0967" n="653" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg653"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/1239</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Counselor for
                        the Department of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">German Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-12-21">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">December 21, 1914</hi>.</date> [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Received December 22</hi>.] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">J. NO. A 3180]</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My dear Mr</hi>. LANSING: I learn that the British S. S. <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mallina</hi>
                     and 
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Tremeadow</hi>, which served as tenders
                    to British cruisers, now demand to be allowed to coal in Panama and to leave for
                    Australia, alleging that they have ceased to be tenders of British warships. I
                    beg to draw your attention to the fact that, as far as can be seen from here,
                    their case, in the principal points, is identical with the case of the German
                        S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Locksun</hi>.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d1044-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Bernstorff</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1044-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1044-f1-content">
                <p>See the section on treatment of belligerent warships, their crews and tenders, in
                    American ports— Internment of the Geier and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Locksun—ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg583">583</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
